 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-A buyer of computation often cannot see the quality of delivered work and can afford to check only a sample of it. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, and free identity replacement, in a mechanism class without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral before receipts; a failed audit refunds the job's escrow and bans the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free replacement makes the ban costless. The rule we study, R3, checks every job of a new identity while the round's audits allow it, and after that window keeps targeting the newest identities, so a replacement can stay under heavy inspection: persistent age-ranked targeting rather than release by age. We report two registered tests. In the first, R3 passed its rule clause against a sealed attacker family, but the original comparative claim failed because neither baseline met its stipulated vulnerability criterion; an amended claim, registered after development results were seen, passed on the public identity-replacement family under interval procedures admitted by proxy. The second test compares R3 with C$^*$, selected in development from twelve configurations of four verified-history adaptations, at matched budget, payment, tolerance, ban, pacing, and sanctions; decomposes R3 into its three parts; adds stronger attacks; and repeats the B3--R3 comparison on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
